6) Dealing with a cursed saddle
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

A cursed saddle can be extremely frustrating. Firstly, if you
are riding your steed when the cursing occurs, you will not be
able to dismount your steed the usual way. There are a few ways
to do this:

 - Put on a ring of conflict and you will be thrown off
 - Zap a wand (or bell) of opening
 - Get engulfed by a vortex or a purple worm etc.
 - Have monsters kill your steed.

Secondly, you will not be able to normally mount a steed with a
cursed saddle or you will slip while trying. You will need to get
the saddle of your steed so that you can uncurse it. This is made
difficult by the fact that you can't remove the saddle the usual
way. Here's what to do:

 - Have monsters kill your steed, then revive it with a wand or
   spell of undead turning, then uncurse the saddle and reapply it.
 - Polymorph into a monkey or a nymph and steal the saddle.

Thirdly, you will not be able to saddle a monster if the saddle is
cursed. You must uncurse it first.


7) Jousting
~~~~~~~~~~~

If you are riding a steed and you have a lance then you can joust.
2d10 for a successful joust (2d2 if with a secondary weapon). This
bonus can't be doubled. To joust successfully, you need to hit with
a lance in melee while riding (and not fumbling or stunned), and the
chance is dependent on your skill in lance (or, if two-weaponing,
the lower of your skill in two-weapon combat and in lance):20% at
unskilled, 40% at basic, 60% at skilled, 80% at expert. The monster
will be stunned for a turn and thrown back a square (if possible).
There is a small chance that the lance will break (dependent on your
Luck). Successfully jousting will always exercise your weapon skill.


